mmitter":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/commit/3ce6929607baf56b5db22c580e5b696a6719186b","node_id":"C_kwDOCePx2toAKDNjZTY5Mjk2MDdiYWY1NmI1ZGIyMmM1ODBlNWI2OTZhNjcxOTE4NmI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/commit/3345c32ce608cd9f25685b6a312c504de7543bfa","sha":"3345c32ce608cd9f25685b6a312c504de7543bfa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/commits/3345c32ce608cd9f25685b6a312c504de7543bfa"}],"sha":"3ce6929607baf56b5db22c580e5b696a6719186b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/commits/3ce6929607baf56b5db22c580e5b696a6719186b"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/26084581?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/samuelmale","id":26084581,"login":"samuelmale","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI2MDg0NTgx","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/samuelmale","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/commits/3345c32ce608cd9f25685b6a312c504de7543bfa/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-29T10:00:38Z","email":"samuelsmalek@gmail.com","name":"Samuel Male"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-29T10:00:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(eqa): score and keep each panel sample on its own (OGC-1243, OGC-1244) (#4463)\n\n* fix(eqa): score each panel sample against its own target (OGC-1243)\n\nA provider panel can carry several samples of one test, the normal design\nfor a multi-level quantitative panel. The provider''s result row was one per\ntest per laboratory, so the second sample''s value overwrote the first, and\nboth were judged against whichever sample''s target was read last.\n\n- eqa_result gains eqa_panel_sample_id (qa/047). Uniqueness becomes two\n  partial indexes: one row per panel sample, or one per test for a result\n  that answers no sample (V1 rows, untargeted tests).\n- Enter results shows one row per panel sample, labelled with its code, and\n  posts panelSampleId. A value that does not name its sample is refused when\n  the test has more than one.\n- Peer statistics are grouped by panel sample, and each result is judged\n  against its own sample''s target and range.\n- The scores CSV gains a trailing sample_code column, and so does the FHIR\n  score Observation (an identifier). The export bundle importer and the FHIR\n  participant-report reader match a row by sample code first, then by analyte\n  name; a name shared by two samples is reported back instead of guessed.\n- The follow-up register and participant history name the sample.\n\n* fix(eqa): keep each panel sample as its own participant result (OGC-1244)\n\nAn external PT result was one row per analyte per round, so when a\nlaboratory received two panel samples of one test, the second sample''s\nvalue was dropped and the provider''s score for one sample landed on the\nother. Follow-up then showed one sample''s value against a verdict judged on\nthe other, and never a target.\n\n- eqa_participant_result gains provider_sample_code and provider_target\n  (qa/047). Its external-PT unique index becomes one row per analyte per\n  provider sample, with COALESCE so an order that names no provider sample\n  keeps the one-per-analyte rule.\n- The result bridge finds a row by the analysis that produced it, then by\n  the provider sample code entered with the order, instead of taking the\n  first row for the analyte. recordAnalyst is unchanged.\n- The export bundle and the participant FHIR Observation carry the sample\n  code. Score intake (CSV and FHIR) matches by sample code, and refuses a\n  score that names no sample once an analyte has two samples in the cycle,\n  scored or not.\n- Score intake keeps the provider''s target, and This Lab''s Follow-Up shows\n  it with the sample code.\n\n* style: reflow the CLAUDE.md worktree note to the formatter''s line breaks\n\nThe worktree note added in #4382 is not wrapped the way spotless wraps\nMarkdown, so `mvn spotless:check` fails on every branch cut from develop.\nThis is exactly the change `mvn spotless:apply` makes.\n\n* test(e2e): expect the scores CSV''s sample_code column (OGC-1243)\n\nThe provider cycle spec pins the scores CSV header, which now ends with sample_code.","tree":{"sha":"3118b2567e0cae953a292e4e17e5e694a186b2da","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/git/trees/3118b2567e0cae953a292e4e17e5e694a186b2da"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/DIGI-UW/OpenELIS-Global-2/git/commits/3345c32ce608cd9f25685b6a312c504de7543bfa","verification":{"payload":"tree 3118b2567e0cae953a292e4e17e5e694a186b2da\nparent b891bbbf6c72f29a690067c98416f3a5bad2ca4c\nauthor Samuel Male <samuelsmalek@gmail.com> 1790676038 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790676038 +0300\n\nfix(eqa): score and keep each panel sample on its own (OGC-1243, OGC-1244) (#4463)\n\n* fix(eqa): score each panel sample against its own target (OGC-1243)\n\nA provider panel can carry several samples of one test, the normal design\nfor a multi-level quantitative panel. The provider''s result row was one per\ntest per laboratory, so the second sample''s value overwrote the first, and\nboth were judged against whichever sample''s target was read last.\n\n- eqa_result gains eqa_panel_sample_id (qa/047). Uniqueness becomes two\n  partial indexes: one row per panel sample, or one per test for a result\n  that answers no sample (V1 rows, untargeted tests).\n- Enter results shows one row per panel sample, labelled with its code, and\n  posts panelSampleId. A value that does not name its sample is refused when\n  the test has more than one.\n- Peer statistics are grouped by panel sample, and each result is judged\n  against its own sample''s target and range.\n- The scores CSV gains a trailing sample_code column, and so does the FHIR\n  score Observation (an identifier). The export bundle importer and the FHIR\n  participant-report reader match a row by sample code first, then by analyte\n  name; a name shared by two samples is reported back instead of guessed.\n- The follow-up register and participant history name the sample.\n\n* fix(eqa): keep each panel sample as its own participant result (OGC-1244)\n\nAn external PT result was one row per analyte per round, so when a\nlaboratory received two panel samples of one test, the second sample''s\nvalue was dropped and the provider''s score for one sample landed on the\nother. Follow-up then showed one sample''s value against a verdict judged on\nthe other, and never a target.\n\n- eqa_participant_result gains provider_sample_code and provider_target\n  (qa/047). Its external-PT unique index becomes one row per analyte per\n  provider sample, with COALESCE so an order that names no provider sample\n  keeps the one-per-analyte rule.\n- The result bridge finds a row by the analysis that produced it, then by\n  the provider sample code entered with the order, instead of taking the\n  first row for the analyte. recordAnalyst is unchanged.\n- The export bundle and the participant FHIR Observation carry the sample\n  code. Score intake (CSV and FHIR) matches by sample code, and refuses a\n  score that names no sample once an analyte has two samples in the cycle,\n  scored or not.\n- Score intake keeps the provider''s target, and This Lab''s Follow-Up shows\n  it with the sample code.\n\n* style: reflow the CLAUDE.md worktree note to the formatter''s line breaks\n\nThe worktree note added in #4382 is not wrapped the way spotless wraps\nMarkdown, so `mvn spotless:check` fails on every branch cut from develop.\nThis is exactly the change `mvn spotless:apply` makes.\n\n* test(e2e): expect the scores CSV''s sample_code column (OGC-1243)\n\nThe provider cycle spec pins the scores CSV header, which now ends with sample_code.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqu4xGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAIXoQADM7JfZrHowNK32MooAt+jMC\nDq4z9YNUSWSvPOCQ4uDA0h3eSaFyP2ejsBGrjDduuzRZ+hHGFJ8nXalLetWwwmBi\nTmPZyVl09NH3pAuF5nSolCZpIk58LT9FtkIsDN+GVASNirgSd3HBhKPeWgE0Y+pN\ny50Q0vOFOhuP34GSOd+w7OtsK244TNNfX2fYAzvR5sCxXIaUrDEpR6lQISCjrBWw\nfpG0RJFYM43VbymXByEAxXGmIsCaYxrIfAjTSS3DDCzXvm1FyBoFYdPvmcRQZK3e\nKwBWfSqzRX34g97njvYTMEaxijwWId/rOp9tPcJryUxNevdD5G+qclyojtjJhyD7\n95E+VI+LQjcDMfOcXTRYvSB/c1ECvDT9uSjxEbbJMmVPZTd53GK8TYm7b+WV4JiR\njvnqSVU1k3pcyknHQwKhvjslQERRR